content.thinking:
The manual regex matches `const commitEdit = (edit) => {`! But `extractFunction` returns NULL. So `m` is truthy, but `matchBracePair` returns -1, causing null. Let me check `matchBracePair`.

`matchBracePair(src, openIdx)`:
```js
function matchBracePair(src, openIdx) {
  let depth = 0;
  for (let i = openIdx; i < src.length; i++) {
    const ch = src[i];
    if (ch === '{') depth++;
    else if (ch === '}') {
      if (--depth === 0) return i;
    }
  }
  return -1;
}
```

In `extractFunction`:
```js
const open = source.indexOf('{', m.index);
const close = matchBracePair(source, open);
```

`m.index` is the start index of the match (the `const` part, maybe offset by whitespace). `source.indexOf('{', m.index)` finds the first `{` at or after m.index. But the match text is `const commitEdit = (edit) => {`. The first `{` in that match is the function body brace. But wait — could there be an earlier `{` between m.index and the function brace? The match starts at `const commitEdit`. Before the body `{`, the match is `const commitEdit = (edit) => ` — no `{`. So `indexOf('{', m.index)` returns the body brace. Good.

Then `matchBracePair(source, open)` counts braces from `open`. It starts depth=0, at `open` ch='{' → depth=1. Then counts until depth returns to 0. The function commitEdit contains nested braces? commitEdit body:
```
const commitEdit = (edit) => {
    const li = edit.closest('li');
    const idx = indexOfLi(li);
    if (idx < 0) return;
    const title = edit.value.trim();
    if (title === '') {
      todos.splice(idx, 1);
    } else {
      todos[idx].title = title;
    }
    save();
    render();
  };
```
This has balanced braces: outer `{`, inner `if (title === '') { ... }` and `else { ... }`. The `matchBracePair` should find the matching `}` for the outer `{`. That's the `}` at the end before `;`. So it should return a valid index, not -1.

